# Letora — Property Operating System

## Product Overview
Letora is a UK-based agentic AI SaaS for solo landlords and small 
letting agencies. It automates: tenant onboarding, rent tracking, 
arrears chasing, maintenance triage, contract generation, lead 
qualification, and listing creation using autonomous AI agents.

## Primary User
A solo landlord managing 10–30 UK properties without staff. 
Needs agents to act, not just suggest. Approves decisions via 
a simple dashboard. Uses WhatsApp and email daily.

## Tech Stack (Never Deviate)
- Framework: Next.js 16 App Router with TypeScript (strict mode)
- Styling: Tailwind CSS + shadcn/ui components only
- Database: Supabase (PostgreSQL) with Row Level Security on ALL tables
- Auth: Supabase Auth
- Icons: Lucide React
- Forms: React Hook Form + Zod
- LLMs: GPT-4o for general tasks, Claude 3.5 Sonnet for legal/contracts
- Email: Resend
- Frontend host: Vercel

## Code Standards
- TypeScript everywhere — zero `any` types
- Server Actions for all Supabase data fetching (never client-side)
- React Hook Form + Zod for every form and schema validation
- kebab-case for all file and folder names
- camelCase for all functions, variables, and props
- Every Supabase table MUST have RLS policies before use
- All agent outputs stored to Supabase before displaying to user
- Use `cn()` from @/lib/utils for all conditional classNames
- Always use Next.js App Router — never pages router
- Always use server components by default, client components only when needed

## Project Structure
src/
  app/
    (auth)/          — login, signup pages
    (dashboard)/     — all authenticated pages
      dashboard/     — main overview
      properties/    — property management
      tenants/       — tenant profiles
      rent/          — rent tracker
      maintenance/   — maintenance requests
      contracts/     — contract management
      leads/         — lead pipeline
  components/
    ui/              — shadcn components (never modify these)
    dashboard/       — dashboard-specific components
    agents/          — agent output display components
  lib/
    supabase/        — client.ts and server.ts
    utils.ts         — cn() and helpers
  types/             — all TypeScript interfaces

## Database Tables
- properties
- tenants
- tenancies
- rent_payments
- maintenance_requests
- leads
- agent_activity_log

## UK Compliance (Flag These Always)
- Right to Rent checks required for all new tenant onboarding
- All tenant documents stored for minimum 1 year post-tenancy
- Renters Rights Act 2026 logic must be commented clearly
- GDPR: No PII logged to console or external services

## Naming Conventions
- DB tables: snake_case plural
- React components: PascalCase
- API routes: /api/agents/[agent-name]
- Agent names: ListingAgent, LeadAgent, ArrearsAgent, 
  ContractAgent, MaintenanceAgent, OnboardingAgent

## What NOT to Do
- Never use pages/ router
- Never use useEffect for data fetching
- Never store API keys in client components
- Never skip RLS policies
- Never build two features in one prompt
- Never use any as a TypeScript type
